    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-19-877-2015",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Precipitation events are expected to become substantially more intense under global warming, but few global comparisons of observations and climate model simulations are available to constrain predictions of future changes in precipitation extremes. We present a systematic global-scale comparison of changes in historical (1901\u20132010) annual-maximum daily precipitation between station observations (compiled in HadEX2) and the suite of global climate models contributing to the fifth phase of the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project (CMIP5). We use both parametric and non-parametric methods to quantify the strength of trends in extreme precipitation in observations and models, taking care to sample them spatially and temporally in comparable ways. We find that both observations and models show generally increasing trends in extreme precipitation since 1901, with the largest changes in the deep tropics. Annual-maximum daily precipitation (Rx1day) has increased faster in the observations than in most of the CMIP5 models. On a global scale, the observational annual-maximum daily precipitation has increased by an average of 5.73 mm over the last 110 years, or 8.5% in relative terms. This corresponds to an increase of 10% K\u22121 in global warming since 1901, which is larger than the average of climate models, with 8.3% K\u22121. The average rate of increase in extreme precipitation per K of warming in both models and observations is higher than the rate of increase in atmospheric water vapor content per K of warming expected from the Clausius\u2013Clapeyron equation. We expect our findings to help inform assessments of precipitation-related hazards such as flooding, droughts and storms.",
    "field": "Climatology of precipitation extremes",
    "problem": "Existing climate models often underestimate observed increases in extreme precipitation, but few global comparisons are available to constrain predictions due to challenges in reconciling disparate spatial and temporal scales between simulations and station data.",
    "method": "The proposed method compares trends in annual-maximum daily precipitation between observations (HadEX2) and climate models (CMIP5) by subsampling the models to match the spatio-temporal coverage of the observations and applying both parametric and non-parametric statistical methods to quantify historical changes.",
    "task": "The task is to systematically compare historical (1901\u20132010) global trends in extreme daily precipitation between station observations and CMIP5 climate model simulations to quantify and contrast their strength and relationship to global warming.",
